export const OVERVIEW = "ardia browse - agent-browser's commands, driving the Ardia you're running\n\nUsage: ardia browse <command> [args] [options]\n\nSame commands, flags and output (text and --json) as agent-browser, so an agent that\nknows `agent-browser <args>` can type `ardia browse <args>`. It drives the running Ardia\nover the ardia command's control connection (Settings > \"Let the ardia command control\nArdia\"), with a small CDP client of its own. Input is instant unless --humanize. Actions print what they changed\n(nodes with refs, URL, title, the POSTs they caused), a session works in background tabs of its\nown (`tab active` picks the user's tab), and text output stops at about 12,000 characters (narrow it\nwith snapshot -i or -s <css>).\n\nCore Commands:\n  here [--read]              Pick the tab the user is looking at and list what to act on (snapshot -i), in one call\n  open [url]                 Open url in a new background tab of this session (later opens reuse it)\n  read [url...]              Read pages' text in background tabs (--links <pattern>: every matching link; -s sel)\n  read-urls <url...>         Fetch pages in parallel from this tab, logged in as it is, without navigating\n  links [pattern]            List the session tab's links whose URL contains pattern (read --links … --list)\n  click <sel>                Click element (or @ref) [--new-tab] [--touch]\n  dblclick <sel>             Double-click element\n  type <sel> <text>          Type into element\n  fill <sel> <text>          Clear, then type (date/time/color/range inputs: set the value; a <select>: pick;\n                             a grid cell: open its editor, set, Enter)\n  press [sel] <key>          Press key (Enter, Tab, Control+a, Meta+a), in <sel> if given\n  keydown <key> / keyup <key>  Hold or release a key\n  keyboard type <text>       Type at the current focus (humanized keystrokes)\n  keyboard inserttext <text> Insert text without key events\n  hover <sel>                Hover element; lists the menus and tooltips it reveals with refs\n  focus <sel>                Focus element\n  check <sel> / uncheck <sel>  Set a checkbox\n  select <sel> <val...>      Pick option(s): a <select>, or a combobox/typeahead (types, picks the match)\n  drag <src> <dst>           Drag onto dst's far half, taking its place [--before|--after] [--synthetic]\n  edit-cell <cell> <value>   Edit a grid cell in one step: open its editor, set, commit [--commit enter|tab]\n  fill-otp <code>            Fill a one-time code into split boxes or a one-time-code field [--profile name]\n  upload <sel> <files...>    Set a file input's files\n  download <sel> <path>      Click element, save the download it starts to path\n  scroll [dir] [px]          Scroll (up/down/left/right, default down 300) [-s <sel>]\n  scrollintoview <sel>       Scroll element into view\n  wait <sel|ms>              Wait for element or time; --text, --url, --load, --fn, --state\n  screenshot [sel] [path]    Screenshot [--full] [--annotate] [--if-changed] [--with-snapshot]\n  pdf <path>                 Save as PDF\n  snapshot                   Accessibility tree with refs [-i] [-c] [-d n] [-s sel] [-u] [--diff] [--diff-new] [--all]\n  snapshot find <query>      Matching snapshot lines with 3 lines around each and where they sit\n  eval <js>                  Run JavaScript in the page [-b base64] [--stdin] [--timeout 2m] [--isolated]\n  close [--all]              Close the tabs this session opened and end the session\n\nSelectors:   CSS, @e3, text=Save (text=\"Save\" exact), role=button[name=\"Save\"], li:has-text(\"Ana\"), li >> nth=2 (third match; -1 last), main >> text=Save, li:text-is(\"Ana\"), xpath=//a;\n             [role=main] also matches <main> and the other implicit roles\nNavigation:  back, forward, reload, pushstate <url>\nAliases:     goto, navigate, visit = open; refresh = reload; evaluate, js = eval; snap = snapshot; shot = screenshot;\n             key = press; quit, exit = close   (a mistyped command or flag says the closest one)\nGet Info:    get text|html|value|title|url|count|box|styles <sel> | attr <sel> <name> (or -s <sel>)\nCheck State: is visible|hidden|enabled|disabled|checked|unchecked|editable|required <sel>\nForms:       form [-s <sel>] [--all]   (every field: label, type, value, checked, required, invalid, ref)\nExtract:     query <sel> [--fields name=h2,price=.price,url=a@href] [--limit n]   (one row per match; tables by header)\nFind:        find role|text|label|placeholder|alt|title|testid|first|last|nth <value> [action] [text]\n             actions: click (default), fill, type, check, uncheck, select, hover, focus, dblclick, text,\n             is checked|unchecked|disabled|enabled|visible|hidden|editable|required\n             find role <role> <action> --name <name>: exact name first, several matches list refs\n             --nth <n> picks match n of several (from 0)\nMouse:       mouse move <x> <y> | down [btn] | up [btn] | wheel <dy> [dx]\nSettings:    set viewport <w> <h> [scale] | reset                 (this session's own hidden tab, off-screen)\n             set device|geo|offline|headers|credentials|media|permission\nNetwork:     network requests [--reload --filter --type --method --status --clear] | request <id>\n             network har start [--content text|all|none] | har stop [path]\n             network route <url> [--abort|--body <json>] | unroute [url]\nStorage:     storage local|session [get [key] | set <k> <v> | clear]\n             cookies [get|set|clear]\n             state save|load <path>\nTabs:        tab [list] | tab new [--label name] [url] | tab <t1|tab-…|label|targetId> | tab active\n             tab close [tab] (only tabs this session opened) | window new [url]\n             (t1 ids are the same in every session; tab-… ids are the ones `ardia windows` prints)\nFrames:      frame <sel|@ref|name|url> | frame main\nDialogs:     dialog status | accept [text] | dismiss\nDiff:        diff snapshot [--baseline f] | diff screenshot --baseline f | diff url <u1> <u2>\nDebug:       console [--clear], errors [--clear] [--uncaught]   (turn on Runtime for the tab on first use)\n             issues [--kind cookie,cors,csp,mixed] [--clear]   (the page's DevTools Issues, explained)\n             styles <sel> [--property p,q] [--all]   (the winning CSS rule for each property)\n             logpoint add <file:line> <expr> | list | rm <id> | clear | scripts   (logs without pausing; own tabs only)\n             highlight <sel>, clipboard read|write|copy|paste\n             trace start|stop [path], profiler start|stop [path]\n             record start <path.webm> [url] | stop | restart             (needs ffmpeg)\n             record [--save <flow>] [--timeout 2m]   (what you do in the tab, as a replayable flow)\nStreaming:   stream enable [--port n] [--input] | disable | status\nRaw CDP:     cdp <Domain.method> [--params json|-] [--param k=v|k:=json] [--target browser|<tab>]\nReact:       react tree | inspect <id> | renders start|stop | suspense [--only-dynamic]\n             (needs `open --enable react-devtools <url>`)\nPerformance: vitals [url] | perf trace [--duration 5s] [--reload] [--out f] | perf insight [--reload] [--crux] [--trace f]\nAudit:       a11y [url] [--tags t1,t2] [--selector css]\nInit:        addinitscript <js> | removeinitscript <id>   (page main world, by request)\nBatch:       batch [--bail] [--out <file>] [\"cmd args\" | '[\"cmd\",\"arg\"]' ...]  (or one command per stdin line; # comments)\nScript:      script [file.js] [--timeout 90s]   (JS on stdin: loops over pages with page.goto/click/text, read())\nNotes:       notes [get|set|append|list|rm] [site] [text|-]   (per-site hints, shown atop snapshot)\n             notes approve <site>   (you, at a terminal: trust new notes before 3 clean visits do)\nFlows:       flow steps | save <name> [--param k=v] | run <name> [--param k=v] [--fields a,b] [--where f=v] [--limit n]\n             flow list [site] | show <name> | rm <name>   (do a task once, then rerun it as one request)\n             flow heal <name> [--apply] [--step n --selector s]   (fix a replayed step that stopped matching)\nSite tools:  tools | tool <name> '{json}' --yes   (the page's WebMCP tools; their output is untrusted)\nAuth Vault:  auth save <name> --url <https url> --username <u> [--password-stdin] [--totp-stdin]\n             auth totp <name>   (the current one-time code; waits for a fresh window under 5 s)\n             auth login <name> [--username-selector s] [--password-selector s] [--submit-selector s]\n             auth login <name> --credential-provider <plugin> [--item ref] [--url url]\n             auth list | show <name> | delete <name>\nPlugins:     plugin add <npm-name[@version] | owner/repo[#ref]> [--name n] [--capability c]\n             plugin list | show <name> | remove <name> | run <name> <type> [--payload json]\nSessions:    session | session info | session list | session id [--scope cwd|worktree] [--prefix p]\n\nOptions:\n  --session <name>        Command-state namespace: its own tabs, refs and captures (AGENT_BROWSER_SESSION);\n                          left out, each agent and worktree gets its own automatic session\n                          (ARDIA_BROWSE_AUTO_SESSION=0 for the shared default)\n  --namespace <name>      Separate set of sessions (AGENT_BROWSER_NAMESPACE)\n  --json                  JSON output (AGENT_BROWSER_JSON)\n  --humanize              Humanized mouse, scroll and typing (AGENT_BROWSER_HUMANIZE=1 for all)\n  --no-humanize           Instant input (the default; AGENT_BROWSER_NO_HUMANIZE=1 forces it)\n  --no-changes            Don't report what an action changed (skips its snapshots and network watch)\n  --no-text               Leave the page text (`text:` lines) out of an action's change report\n  --data-dir <dir>        Ardia data dir to find (default $ARDIA_DATA_DIR, else the installed app's)\n  --port <n>, --cdp <n>   A plain DevTools test server on 127.0.0.1:n (testing only)\n  --ws <url>              A browser WebSocket URL\n  --capture-console       With open: start console and error capture before the page loads\n  --enable react-devtools With open: install the React DevTools hook before page JS\n  --init-script <path>    With open: register a page init script (repeatable)\n  --annotate              Screenshot: draw numbered labels for the refs\n  --screenshot-dir/-format/-quality   Screenshot defaults (AGENT_BROWSER_SCREENSHOT_*)\n  --max-output <chars>    Cap text output (default 12000, 20000 for script, 0 for none; AGENT_BROWSER_MAX_OUTPUT)\n  --full                  Don't cap text output\n  --no-content-boundaries Leave page output unwrapped; by default it sits between untrusted-content\n                          markers with a fresh random nonce (AGENT_BROWSER_CONTENT_BOUNDARIES=0)\n  --no-auto-dialog        Don't accept alert/beforeunload dialogs (AGENT_BROWSER_NO_AUTO_DIALOG)\n  --pin-tab, --no-pin-tab Fail with tab_gone instead of falling back when the session's tab closes\n  --idle-timeout <time>   End an idle session after 10s, 3m, 1h or raw ms (default 1h)\n\nNeed Ardia 0.2.49 or later: cookies, state save (and state load of a file with cookies), set device|geo|\noffline|headers|credentials|media|permission, network route, upload, download, highlight, clipboard, trace,\nprofiler, record and stream enable. From 0.2.49 the ardia command is a full DevTools client of Ardia;\nan older Ardia keeps it to a short list of methods, so there they fail with policy_refused naming the\nmethod and what to do instead, and updating Ardia fixes it. A `cdp` method an older Ardia refuses\nfails the same way.\nset viewport works on a tab this session opened and never showed.\n\nNot here: connect, inspect, install, upgrade, doctor, dashboard, chat (no AI), mcp, profiles,\nconfirm/deny, iOS (-p ios, swipe, tap), cloud providers, and launch options (--headed,\n--profile, --proxy, --extension, --args, ...), since Ardia is the browser you're running.\n\nExit status: 0 done, 1 the command failed, 2 Ardia can't be reached or closed the connection\n(the outcome of an interrupted action is reported as unknown), 130 interrupted.\n\nExamples:\n  ardia browse open example.com\n  ardia browse snapshot -i\n  ardia browse click @e2\n  ardia browse fill @e3 \"user@example.com\"\n  ardia browse fill @e7 2026-11-09\n  ardia browse batch <<'EOF'\n  click @e10\n  fill #name \"Ana Ruiz\"\n  click #save\n  EOF\n  ardia browse find role button click --name Submit\n  ardia browse read --links /p/ -s table.specs\n  ardia browse script <<'EOF'\n  const out = [];\n  for (const url of await page.links(\"/p/\")) { await page.goto(url); out.push({ name: await page.text(\"h1\") }); }\n  return out;\n  EOF\n  ardia browse screenshot --annotate page.png\n";
export const COMMANDS: Readonly<Record<string, string>> = {
  "open": "ardia browse open [url] [--read] [--enable react-devtools] [--init-script <path>] [--capture-console] [--label name] [--here]\n\n--read prints the page's full text, as `read` does, in place of the change report, so opening a page to read\nit is one call.\n\nWithout a tab, opens a new background tab in Ardia's sidebar (the user's own tab is never navigated\nunless the session picked it with `tab <id>`). When the session is using the user's tab (`tab active`), open goes to a new background tab of its own and its\nfirst line says so, leaving the user's tab as it was; open --here <url> navigates the picked tab instead.\nA ref from the page a later open replaced fails as stale and names both pages; `tab new <url>` keeps both. Later opens navigate the\nsession's tab. A URL without a\nscheme gets https:// (http:// for localhost). Prints the title and URL, then the page it landed on as a\nnavigating click reports it (interactive nodes with refs and the main text with refs inline), so no\nsnapshot is needed; --no-changes prints only the title and URL.",
  "snapshot": "ardia browse snapshot [-i] [-c] [-d <n>] [-s <sel>] [-u] [--diff | --diff-new] [--all] [--with-screenshot <png>]\nardia browse snapshot find <query | /pattern/> [-i] [-s <sel>] [--all]\n\nAccessibility tree with refs (@e1, @e2...). -i interactive only, -c compact, -d depth, -s scope to a\nselector or @ref (every match that shows, so hidden matches like a closed cookie banner are skipped),\n-u include link URLs, --diff only what changed since this session's last snapshot\nof the document, --diff-new only the controls and text that appeared since then. Date and time inputs show as one textbox with their value. Names past 100\ncharacters end in … (find --name takes the clipped name as a prefix). In a long run of one role, -i\nlists the enabled members and counts the disabled ones in one line. Site notes, when there\nare any, come first.\n\nWhen a modal dialog is open on top of the page (found by what is under five points of it, not by z-index),\nthe snapshot shows only that dialog and says so first; --all (or --no-modal-scope) shows the whole page.\nText the page hides with opacity 0 or inside a collapsed box is left out, single-child wrappers fold into\ntheir child, a name that repeats its own text is shown once, and an icon-only or repeated control gets a\nshort hint (icon, img, testid or near) so it can be told apart.\n\nLabel text that repeats the field it names, text that repeats a field's value, and the wrappers around\na list's options and a table's rows are left out; --full keeps every node.\n\nOutput fits the output cap (--max-output, 12,000 characters by default) line by line: controls first, then\nheadings, labels and landmarks, then the rest of the text, and a last line counts what was left out.\nA line that was left out never gives a ref. --full shows every line.\n\nfind searches the snapshot's lines (case aside; /pattern/ for a regular expression) and prints each match\nwith 3 lines around it and the path of containers it sits in; only the refs it prints are given.\n--with-screenshot <png> saves a screenshot of the viewport taken with the snapshot from the same page state,\nand --json lists each ref's box in the image's pixels.\n\nRefs belong to this snapshot's document: refs inside a cross-process frame read f1e7, f2e9 and work like\nany other. A ref from a page that navigated or was replaced fails with vocab_superseded; a ref whose\nelement left the same page fails with stale_ref. --json's epoch counts the page's vocabularies: it goes up\nwhen the page navigates or redraws with nothing in common with the last snapshot. When the page redraws an element, its ref follows the new one only if\nit was the only one with its role and name, and still is, inside the same named dialog, row or form.\n--tools adds a # site tools line: the WebMCP tools the page registers (document.modelContext,\nnavigator.modelContext, form[toolname]), their arguments, and [CONSEQUENTIAL] on ones that may change things.",
  "click": "ardia browse click <sel> [--new-tab] [--button left|right|middle] [--touch] [--humanize] [--no-changes] [--no-text]\n\nClicks the element (with --humanize along a curved mouse path with human timing) and prints what\nchanged: nodes added, changed or removed with their refs, URL and title, and non-GET requests it\ncaused. A long run of one role (options, links, many buttons) is one line with its count, first and\nlast name and refs, plus any selected one; a run with disabled members lists the enabled ones and\ncounts the rest; nodes redrawn unchanged are one line with their new refs. After a navigation, or when\nthe click opens something (a heading, dialog, article or region), `text:` lines give its readable\ntext with refs inline (`[@e5]`), images as `[image \"…\": screenshot @eN]` and frames with their text;\n--no-text leaves them out.\nFails with `covered by <...>` when another element sits on top of the target. A disabled target is\nwaited for up to 1.5 s (it fails then if it's still disabled), so a dependent field can follow the\none that enables it in the same batch.\n--touch taps instead when the page supports touch (it reports touch points), and clicks otherwise, saying so.\nThe selector can also come as -s <sel> or --selector <sel>.",
  "fill": "ardia browse fill <sel> <text> [--humanize] [--no-changes]\n\nFocuses the field, clears it (select all, Backspace), then puts the text in: a textarea, an editable\nelement or a plain text/search/email/url/tel/number input gets it in one insert (input events, no key\nevents); a combobox, a password or --humanize types per character. Date, time, datetime-local, month,\nweek, color and range inputs get their value set instead (YYYY-MM-DD, HH:MM or 2:30 PM,\nYYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM, YYYY-MM, YYYY-Www, #rrggbb, a number), with input and change events; a bad value\nfails with the format. On a <select> it picks the option whose label or value is the text (else the\nonly one containing it). On a combobox it waits briefly for suggestions and says how many show; typing\nnever picks one (use select for that). A disabled field is waited for up to 1.5 s.\n\nSpreadsheet grids: on a cell ([role=gridcell], [role=cell] or td, or anything inside one) that isn't a\nfield, it double-clicks the cell (Enter if that opens nothing), waits up to 1 s for the editor, sets it\n(picks on a <select>, datalist or combobox editor), presses Enter to commit and prints the cell's new\nvalue: ~ gridcell \"Reorder level, FAS-53565 Reno\": 250 (was 150). A cell that already holds a field is\nfilled in place. When the grid keeps the editor open (a rejected value), it presses Escape and fails with\nthe grid's message. Put every cell in one batch:\n  fill \"#c-2356-reorder\" 250\n  fill \"#c-n1-category\" Fasteners\nPrints what changed. The selector can also come as -s <sel> or --selector <sel>, before the text.\nA plain label works as the selector too: when it matches no element, the field labelled, named or with\nthat placeholder is used: `fill \"Full name\" \"Ana Ruiz\"` (also select, check and uncheck).",
  "select": "ardia browse select <sel> <value...> [--humanize] [--no-changes]\n\nOn a <select>, picks the options by value or label. On a combobox (a typeahead or chip input, a\nbutton that opens a listbox, an input with a datalist) it does it per value: types the value (or\nopens the list), waits for the suggestions to settle, and clicks the one that matches: the exact\ntext, else one whose line or email is the value, else the only one containing it. Two matches or\nnone fail and list the suggestions, nothing picked. Several values fill a chip input in one call:\n  ardia browse select @e5 \"Priya Raman\" alex@example.com\nPrints what it picked and what changed. A disabled target is waited for up to 1.5 s.\nThe selector can be the field's label: `select Country Mexico`.",
  "type": "ardia browse type <sel> <text> [--no-humanize]\n\nTypes into the element without clearing it.",
  "press": "ardia browse press [<sel>] <key> [--no-humanize]\n\nWith <sel>, focuses that element first. Keys: Enter, Tab, Escape, Backspace, Delete, Space, Arrow*, Home, End, PageUp, PageDown, F1-F12.\nModifiers: Control, Alt, Shift, Meta. Control or Meta with a selects all, with z undoes and with y or Shift+z redoes;\non macOS Meta+c/x/v, Meta and Alt with the arrows, Backspace and Delete also edit as they do in a text field.",
  "keyboard": "ardia browse keyboard type <text> | inserttext <text>\n\ntype sends humanized keystrokes to the focused element; inserttext inserts at once.",
  "scroll": "ardia browse scroll [up|down|left|right] [px] [-s <sel>] [--no-humanize]\n\nWheel scrolling with momentum, at the mouse position (moved into the page or the container first).",
  "wait": "ardia browse wait <sel|ms> [--state visible|hidden|attached|detached] | --text <t> | --url <glob> |\n  --load load|domcontentloaded|networkidle | --fn <js> [--timeout ms]\n\n--fn evaluates in the page's main world, like eval (--main-world says so; --isolated: in an isolated world).\nTimeouts default to 25 s.",
  "screenshot": "ardia browse screenshot [sel] [path | --out path] [--full] [--annotate] [--screenshot-format png|jpeg] [--screenshot-quality n] [--if-changed [--threshold pct]] [--with-snapshot]\n\n--if-changed saves nothing and says so when this view looks the same as at its last screenshot (under --threshold percent of pixels changed, 0.5 by default).\n--annotate takes an interactive snapshot and draws the numbered labels onto the image (nothing is added\nto the page). Without a path it saves under $TMPDIR/ardia-browse/screenshots.\n-s <sel> or --selector <sel> names the element to capture, like [sel].\n--with-snapshot takes the snapshot with the screenshot, from the same page state, and prints it after the saved path;\n--json lists each ref's box in the image's pixels (taken twice at most when the page changes in between).",
  "eval": "ardia browse eval <js> | -b <base64> | --stdin [--timeout 2m] [--out <file>] [--isolated]\n\nWaits up to 60 s for the promise the code returns; --timeout gives it longer, up to 10m.\n\nRuns in the page's main world (the one place, with init scripts and the React hook, that does), where the\npage's own globals are; --main-world says so explicitly. --isolated runs it in an isolated world instead: the\nsame DOM, none of the page's JavaScript, and nothing it defines is visible to the page.\n--out <file> saves the whole value (JSON, or the text as is) and prints a short summary instead.",
  "get": "ardia browse get text|html|value|title|url|count|box|styles <sel> | attr <sel> <name>\n\nattribute works as attr. The selector can also come as -s <sel> or --selector <sel>.\n\ncdp-url works only with --port or --ws: the real URL carries Ardia's token.",
  "find": "ardia browse find <role|text|label|placeholder|alt|title|testid|first|last|nth> <value> [action] [text] [--name n] [--exact|--partial]\n\nActions: click (default), fill, type, press [key] (Enter if none), check, uncheck, select, hover, focus, dblclick,\ntext, and is <state> (checked, unchecked, disabled, enabled, visible, hidden, editable, required), which prints\ntrue or false: `find label \"Accept terms\" is checked`. The found element\ngets the next ref, so later commands can use it.\n\nfind role takes the role and accessible name the snapshot shows, in any order: `find role button\nclick --name Save`, `find role button --name Save click` or `find role button Save click`. A name\nmatches exactly first (case and spacing aside; --exact makes it case-sensitive), else as part of a\nlonger name. When several elements match, nothing is clicked and the error lists them with refs.\nAn element that isn't there yet is waited for up to 1.5 s. find role … --nth <n> picks match n of several\n(from 0, like find nth) and says which; it works only with find role.",
  "tab": "ardia browse tab [list] | new [--label name] [url] | <t1|tab-…|label|targetId> | active | close [tab] [--show]\n\nlist shows the user's tabs too, with the tab-… id `ardia windows` prints when the address pairs them\nup, and which session opened or uses each. A t1 id is the same in every session and never moves to\nanother tab. Switching changes which tab this session acts on, not what Ardia shows (--show does).\nA session never takes the user's tab on its own: until it opens or picks one, commands that need a\npage fail with no_session_tab. `tab active` picks the tab the user is looking at; the session works\nin it but won't navigate it (open goes to a background tab of its own) unless picked by id. close only\ncloses tabs this session opened, and\nnot one another session is using. When Ardia swaps a tab's page (it slept, or crashed and reloaded),\nthe session follows it to the new page.",
  "console": "ardia browse console [--clear]\n\nThe first call turns on the Runtime domain for the session's tab (Chrome then replays earlier\nmessages); until then ardia browse leaves Runtime off, unlike agent-browser.",
  "errors": "ardia browse errors [--clear] [--uncaught]\n\nUncaught exceptions; turns Runtime on like console. --uncaught prints each one with its Error.cause chain\n(5 deep) and every frame mapped to the original file where the script carries an inline (data:) source map and\nthe tab is one this session opened (original file:line:column, then the bundle's position in brackets). Ardia resolves them (devtools.stackTrace)\nwhen it can, else this command does; neither fetches a map from the network.",
  "network": "ardia browse network requests [--reload] [--filter p] [--type t] [--method m] [--status 2xx] [--clear] | request <id>\n  --reload starts recording and reloads the page, so its own loads are listed (--type xhr,fetch: its data calls)\n  | har start [--content text|all|none] | har stop [path] | route <url> [--abort|--body json] | unroute [url]\n\nroute needs Fetch.enable: Ardia 0.2.49 or later (an older one refuses it, policy_refused). Requests, their\ndetails and HAR files leave out Cookie, Cookie2, Set-Cookie, Set-Cookie2, Authorization and Proxy-Authorization\nheaders.",
  "auth": "ardia browse auth save <name> --url <https url> [--origin <url>]... --username <u> [--password-stdin]\n  [--username-selector s] [--password-selector s] [--submit-selector s]\nardia browse auth login <name> | list | show <name> | delete <name>\n\nPasswords live in the macOS login Keychain (via /usr/bin/security, written on its stdin, never argv).\n--password is refused. login checks the page's committed origin and frame against the saved https\norigins before typing and stops if the page navigates, the frame changes or focus leaves the field.",
  "batch": "ardia browse batch [--bail] [--out <file>] [\"cmd args\" | '[\"cmd\",\"arg\"]' ...]\n\n--out <file> saves everything the batch prints and shows which lines failed.\nRuns commands in order in one call; without arguments, one command per stdin line (a heredoc\nworks; blank lines and # lines are skipped). Lines use shell quoting or a JSON array. Each answer,\nwith its changes, is printed in order. --bail stops at the first failure. A line takes its command's\nown flags and per-command ones like --full, --no-changes or --humanize. `close` as the last line ends the\nsession after the rest ran (not after a failure). To loop over pages, use read or script instead.\n\nWhen an earlier line redraws the page and a later line's ref goes stale, the line runs again on the new\nelement if it was the only one with its role and name and one still is, in the same frame and dialog, and says so\nfirst: ↻ @e14→@e352 (--json: `healed`). Only the line's selector changes, never text it types.\nWhen none or several match, the line fails and lists the refs on the page now (--json: `matches`).",
  "notes": "ardia browse notes [get|set|append|list|rm] [site] [text|-]  (also `ardia notes`)\n\nPlain notes per site (host[:port]) for your own agent, in <data dir>/Notes/<site>.md (0600, 8 KB each).\nWithout a site it's the session tab's (--tab <id> for another tab's). - reads stdin. snapshot prints a\nsite's notes first, and so does an action that lands on another site.",
  "flow": "ardia browse flow steps [--all]\nardia browse flow save <name> [--from n] [--to n] [--param k=v]... [--output s2r1] [--description text]\nardia browse flow run <name> [--param k=v]... [--fields a,b.c] [--where field=value]... [--limit n]\n  [--site s] [--timeout 60s]\nardia browse flow list [site] | show <name> [--site s] | rm <name> [--site s]\n\nThe session keeps its last 200 actions with the first-party JSON each GET loaded (and JSON a page embeds:\n__NEXT_DATA__, JSON-LD, window.x = {...}). save turns the steps since the last save (from the last open) into\na read-only flow for the site, in <data dir>/Flows/<site>/<name>.json (0600): GET requests only, no bodies,\ntyped text and query values become params, and a URL keeps only params, numbers and short words.\nrun opens the flow's start page like open (or uses a tab already there) and sends the GET from it, with your\nlogins; it never replays clicks or typing. It checks the answer still has the recorded shape and prints the\nmain list as items. --where keeps items whose field contains the value; unknown fields fail with the valid\nones. Codes: redirected, auth_expired, blocked, rate_limited, drifted, fetch_failed, flow_not_get; then drive\nthe page yourself. open and snapshot list a site's flows once.\n\nFlows v2 replay page actions. `flow save --actions` keeps each click, fill, select and check with ranked\nlocators (aria-label, placeholder, name, test id, visible text, then CSS and XPath), the element's role and\nname, a short intent and the wanted on/off state; a range with no JSON answer saves this way on its own, and\n`record --save` makes one from what you do in the tab. `flow run` replays them: each locator that matches exactly one element, then\nEscape and the locators again, then the role and name; a step every tier misses stops the run and keeps a\nfallback record with the page's snapshot. Replaying page actions needs --yes, since a click can change things, and --replay replays a\nrequest flow's actions. `flow heal <name>` lists those records (--json has them whole), --apply keeps the\nlocators that worked, and --step n --selector s, --step n --role r --name n or --patch file.json set new\nones; generated ids, hashed classes and values from one run are refused (heal_rejected).\n--fixture <dir> (save or run) writes the answer's page or JSON with extract.mjs and extract.test.mjs, so the\nextraction and its shape check run offline with node --test.",
  "read": "ardia browse read [url] --fast [--outline] [--filter <pattern>]\nardia browse read [url...] [--links <pattern>] [-s <selector>] [--stdin] [--limit n] [--timeout ms] [--http] [--list] [--out <file>] [--nav]\nardia browse read [-s <list>] --scroll [--limit rows] [--match <text>] [--timeout ms]\nardia browse read --data [--block n|id] [--fields a,b.c] [--where field=value] [--limit n]\n\n--data lists the JSON this tab's page embeds (__NEXT_DATA__, JSON-LD, application/json scripts, window.x = {...})\nwith each block's main list and fields; --fields/--where/--limit return just those items from the largest block (or --block).\n\nReads each page in a background tab of this session's own (four at a time, through your logins, after its\nscripts ran) and prints its title, URL and text: the page's <main>, else its body, or what -s matches.\nSite menus and link footers outside the main content, and filter lists over 15 lines, print as one line each;\n--nav prints them in full.\nTables and ARIA grids print one row per line, cells split by ` | `, with a note when a grid renders only\nsome of its rows. Tabs close when it's done. With no URL it reads the session's tab. --links <pattern> reads every link whose URL contains the pattern (* matches\nanything), in page order, from each URL you name (opened in a background tab, not read itself), else from the session's\ntab; --list only prints those links. URLs can also come one per line\non stdin (--stdin or -), and /paths resolve against the session's tab. Without --links, every URL you name is read (up to 100);\n--links finds at most 20 unless --limit (100). A PDF prints its text, page by page.\nPages share an output cap of 6,000 characters a page (12,000 to 36,000), and a cut read says how to narrow it\n(-s or a tighter --links) and names the file holding all of it last (--json and > file have all). --out <file> saves every page\n(JSON for a .json file, else text) and prints one line per page.\n--scroll reads a long or virtualized list on the session's tab (-s takes a selector or a ref): it scrolls the\nlist's own scroll box until it stops growing and prints each row once, one per line, in list order, then puts\nthe scroll back. --match keeps rows containing the text (any case); --limit stops after that many rows (1,000;\n10,000 with --match); --timeout is the whole scroll's (30 s). Big output: redirect it to a file and grep it.\n--http fetches outside the browser instead (no logins, no page scripts; faster on public docs), and so do\n--llms index|full, --require-md, --raw, --outline and --filter, which also work on the session's tab's URL.\nExit 1 when any page failed (its block says why).\n\n--fast tries what the site publishes for readers before any scraping: the page as text/markdown (Accept\nheader, then its .md sibling), a matching entry in the nearest llms.txt, then /.well-known/ard.json, and only\nthen the HTML, through the reader, or as the page's whole text when the reader keeps under 25% of it. The\nanswer ends with the method (markdown, llms_txt, ard, readability or raw), also in --json as method.\n--outline and --filter <pattern> narrow any of them. Every probe, a link a page lists and each redirect must stay on the page's origin or reach only public\naddresses (unsafe_address). On the session's tab, --fast falls back to the tab's own text.",
  "script": "ardia browse script [file.js | '<code>'] [--timeout 90s] [--out <file>]   (or the script on stdin)\n\n--out <file> saves the whole returned value (JSON, or a string as is) and prints a summary and the log;\nbuild deliverables from that file instead of copying printed output. `state` is an object the session keeps\nbetween scripts, even past a timeout: push rows to it in long loops and continue in the next script.\n\nRuns a short JavaScript program that drives pages, so a loop over many pages is one call. It runs in a\nsandbox of its own (no files, modules or network), outside the page it drives (no document or window:\nDOM code goes in page.evaluate), and every page action is one of ardia browse's commands on this session (the\nsame tab, refs and rules), so nothing a command refuses works here either.\n\n  page.goto(url)  back()  reload()        page.click(sel)  dblclick(sel)  fill(sel, text)  type(sel, text)\n  page.focus(sel)  press(key)  keyboard(text)   page.select(sel, value)  check(sel)  uncheck(sel)  hover(sel)\n  page.scroll(dir, px)  page.text(sel?)  value(sel)  attr(sel, name)  count(sel)  visible(sel)  url()  title()\n  page.snapshot('-i -s main')  page.links(pattern?)  page.wait(sel | ms | {text, url, load, fn})\n  page.evaluate(fn, ...args) or page.eval(fn, ...args)   (fn runs in the page's main world, like eval; pass\n    the script's values as args, JSON, since fn can't see them)\n  page.$eval(sel, fn, ...args)  page.$$eval(sel, fn, ...args)   (fn gets the first match / every match)\n  page.frames()  -> [{id, url, name, main, crossProcess}], each with evaluate, $eval and $$eval\n  read(urls | {links, selector})  -> [{url, title, text, error}]   (as `read`, in background tabs)\n  browse('tab', 'list')  -> {text, data}   (any other command)\n  log(...) / console.log(...)   sleep(ms)   return value   (printed as JSON)\n\nReads (text, attr, evaluate, $eval, $$eval, read) run together under Promise.all; actions run one at a\ntime and wait for the page to settle, and their answer skips what changed unless the last argument is\n{receipt: true}. A selector is CSS, @eN, text=… or xpath=…; a /path\nresolves against the tab. A failed command throws (err.code, err.message); uncaught, the script stops and\nsays which line, and lists the calls before it with their lines and ✓ or ✗ (--json: `steps`). It stops at --timeout (default 90s, at most 10m), and after any command whose outcome is\nunknown. Output is capped like other commands.\n\n  ardia browse script <<'EOF'\n  const rows = [];\n  for (const url of await page.links('/p/')) {\n    await page.goto(url);\n    rows.push({ name: await page.text('h1'), price: await page.text('.price') });\n  }\n  return rows;\n  EOF",
  "close": "ardia browse close [--all]\n\nCloses only the tabs this session opened (never the browser) and ends the session; a tab another\nsession is using stays open and the answer says so. --all does it for every session.",
  "tools": "ardia browse tools\n\nLists the WebMCP tools the page in the session's tab registers (document.modelContext,\nnavigator.modelContext, form[toolname]) with their arguments. Names and descriptions come from the page and\nare untrusted; [CONSEQUENTIAL] marks tools that may delete, buy, send or otherwise change something, and\n[page says read-only] is only the page's claim.",
  "tool": "ardia browse tool <name> ['{json args}' | -] --yes [--timeout 60s]\n\nCalls a WebMCP tool the page registers, after checking the arguments against its input schema. Every call\nruns the page's own code and only the page says what it does, so it needs --yes (unconfirmed_tool;\nconsequential_tool for a [CONSEQUENTIAL] one: destructive annotation or a name like delete, pay, send, which\na read-only claim doesn't clear). The call goes through the same provider and document it was found in, and is\nrefused if the tool changed in between (site_tool_changed, document_changed). The output is printed as\n[UNTRUSTED] page data; an unknown name fails with no_site_tool.",
  "record": "ardia browse record start <path.webm> [url] | stop | restart <path.webm> [url]\n\nNeeds Page.startScreencast and ffmpeg on PATH. Page.startScreencast needs Ardia 0.2.49 or later; an older\nArdia refuses it (policy_refused), so take screenshots there.\n\nardia browse record [--save <flow-name>] [--timeout 2m] [--idle 20s] [--here] [--description text]\n\nWatches the tab you're on (the session's with --here) while you click, type, pick and send forms, and stops\nafter --timeout (2m, up to 10m) or --idle quiet seconds after the first action (20s). It prints a v2 flow\nwhose steps find each element by its label, placeholder, name, text or role and name, never by a snapshot\nref; typed text becomes params and passwords and other secret fields (latched once seen) become params with no value kept; typed text\na flow can't hold as an example is kept as a param with no example. Runs need --yes. --save keeps it.",
  "stream": "ardia browse stream enable [--port n] [--input] | disable | status\n\nBinds 127.0.0.1 only, with its own random token in the URL; view only unless --input. Needs Page.startScreencast: Ardia 0.2.49 or later (an older one refuses it, policy_refused).",
  "react": "ardia browse react tree | inspect <id> | renders start | renders stop | suspense [--only-dynamic]\n\nNeeds `ardia browse open --enable react-devtools <url>`.",
  "plugin": "ardia browse plugin add <npm-name[@version] | owner/repo[#ref]> [--name n] [--capability c]... | list | show <name>\n  | remove <name> | run <name> <type> [--payload json]\n\nadd pins the exact version (or commit) and installs with npm --ignore-scripts into Ardia's data dir.\nNothing is loaded from the current directory or an agent-browser.json.",
  "session": "ardia browse session | info | list | id [--scope cwd|worktree] [--prefix p]\n\nA session is a command-state namespace (its tabs, refs, mouse position and captures) on the same\nArdia, not an isolated browser: cookies and logins are the user's.",
  "drag": "ardia browse drag <src> <dst> [--before|--after] [--synthetic] [--humanize] [--no-changes]\n\nDrags the source onto the target with the mouse (an HTML5 draggable goes through drag interception). It\ndrops on the target's far half, 75% of its height when moving down and 25% moving up (its width in a row),\nso in a sortable list the source takes the target's place; --before or --after picks the side. When the\nsource sits in a list, the answer names its new place (moved to 5 of 5 (was 4)) or says the order didn't\nchange, and prints what changed (the save request included), so there's no need to read the list again.\nWhen the page offers no drag interception, or its dropzone ignored the native drop, the drag is sent as page\nDragEvents (dragstart, dragenter, dragover, drop, dragend) sharing one DataTransfer; --synthetic does that from\nthe start.",
  "dblclick": "ardia browse dblclick <sel> [--humanize] [--no-changes]\n\nDouble-clicks the element and prints what changed. To edit a grid cell, `fill <cell> <value>` does it in one line.",
  "fill-otp": "ardia browse fill-otp <code> [--within <container>] [--no-changes]\nardia browse fill-otp --profile <name> [--no-changes]\n\nFills a one-time code: a group of 4 to 8 single-character boxes gets one character each, with the input and\nchange events a person's typing sends, or a single field marked autocomplete=\"one-time-code\" gets the whole\ncode. The code is treated as a secret: never printed, masked anywhere else in the output, and the boxes are\nmasked in snapshots. --within limits the search to a container (an @ref, CSS, or text shown in it).\n--profile types the current TOTP code of a saved auth profile, only on that profile's own sites, without the\ncode ever leaving the daemon.",
  "edit-cell": "ardia browse edit-cell <cell> <value> [--commit enter|tab] [--within <container>] [--no-changes]\n\nEdits an ARIA grid cell or table cell in one step: double-clicks it (or presses Enter), finds the inline\n<input>, <textarea> or contenteditable editor it opens, sets the value and commits with Enter (or Tab).\nIt prints the cell's new value and the old one; an editor that stays open (a validation error) is cancelled\nwith Escape and reported.",
  "hover": "ardia browse hover <sel> [--within <container>] [--humanize] [--no-changes]\n\nMoves the mouse onto the element and prints what changed (menus and tooltips it opened).\nTooltips, menus, popovers, listboxes and dialogs the hover reveals within 300 ms are listed under\n`revealed:` with refs for their items, so a hover menu can be clicked without another snapshot.",
  "focus": "ardia browse focus <sel>\n\nFocuses the element (DOM.focus, no click) and prints what changed. Then `keyboard type <text>` types there.",
  "check": "ardia browse check <sel> | uncheck <sel> [--humanize] [--no-changes]\n\nClicks a checkbox, radio or switch (its label when the box itself is hidden) only when it isn't already in\nthat state, and fails when the click didn't change it. A disabled one is waited for up to 1.5 s.\nThe selector can be the box's label: `check \"Accept terms\"`.",
  "uncheck": "ardia browse uncheck <sel> [--humanize] [--no-changes]\n\nLike check: unchecks it when it's checked.",
  "back": "ardia browse back | forward\n\nGoes one step through the tab's history, waits for the page, and prints its URL.",
  "forward": "ardia browse forward | back\n\nGoes one step through the tab's history, waits for the page, and prints its URL.",
  "reload": "ardia browse reload\n\nReloads the session's tab, waits for load, and prints its URL. Refs from before go stale.",
  "is": "ardia browse is visible|enabled|checked <sel>\n\nPrints true or false.",
  "scrollintoview": "ardia browse scrollintoview <sel> [--humanize]\n\nScrolls the element into view (with --humanize, wheel steps).",
  "frame": "ardia browse frame <sel|@ref|name|url> | frame main\n\nMakes later commands act inside that iframe (same-process ones only) until `frame main`.",
  "window": "ardia browse window new [url]\n\nOpens a new Ardia window with one tab of this session's.",
  "storage": "ardia browse storage local|session [get [key] | set <key> <value> | remove <key> | clear]\n\nReads or changes the page's localStorage or sessionStorage (of the selected frame). get without a key\nprints every key: value line.",
  "mouse": "ardia browse mouse move <x> <y> | down [left|right|middle] | up [button] | wheel <dy> [dx] [--humanize]\n\nRaw mouse input at viewport coordinates; a held button stays down until `mouse up`.",
  "keydown": "ardia browse keydown <key> | keyup <key>\n\nHolds a key down until keyup (keys and modifiers as press).",
  "keyup": "ardia browse keyup <key>\n\nReleases a key held with keydown.",
  "upload": "ardia browse upload <sel> <files...>\n\nSets the files of a file input (DOM.setFileInputFiles) and prints what changed. Paths are\nrelative to the current directory; each must be a file. Needs Ardia 0.2.49 or later; an older Ardia refuses it\n(policy_refused), so ask the user to choose the file there.",
  "download": "ardia browse download <sel> <path>\n\nClicks the element and saves the download it starts to path (Browser.setDownloadBehavior).\n`wait --download [path]` waits for the next download that starts, without clicking. Both need Ardia 0.2.49 or\nlater; an older Ardia refuses them (policy_refused). There, for download, the error says how to fetch a public text\nfile instead; for wait --download, ask the user to download it.",
  "cdp": "ardia browse cdp <Domain.method> [--params <json>|-] [--param k=v]... [--param k:=json]... [--target browser|<tab>]\n  [--timeout 30s]\n\nSends one Chrome DevTools Protocol method and prints its result as JSON. It goes to this session's tab, to the\ntab --target names (t1, tab-…, a label or a targetId, as `tab` takes), or with --target browser to the browser\nitself (Browser.*, Target.*, Storage.*, SystemInfo.*). --params - reads the params from stdin (UTF-8, up to\n1 MB). Params must be clean JSON: no NaN or Infinity, no key twice, at most 64 levels, not empty. On your tab\npicked with `tab active`, a call goes only with --target naming it.\n\nCalls run on a DevTools session of their own, so Runtime.disable and the like never touch the session's\nconsole, network or snapshots. Domains enabled through cdp stay on while the daemon stays connected; after\n30 s idle it reconnects and they are off again: --json's data.session is then `reattached` (else `attached`\nor `kept`), and text mode says so. Events aren't printed. The whole result prints, never cut by --max-output.\n\nExamples:\n  ardia browse cdp Page.getLayoutMetrics\n  ardia browse cdp Target.getTargets --target browser\n  ardia browse cdp Runtime.evaluate --param expression=document.title --param returnByValue:=true",
  "cookies": "ardia browse cookies [get] | set <name> <value> [--url u] [--domain d] [--path p] [--httpOnly] [--secure]\n  [--sameSite Strict|Lax|None] [--expires epoch] | set --curl <file> | clear\n\nReads and changes the browser's cookies (Network.getCookies, setCookie, clearBrowserCookies).",
  "set": "ardia browse set viewport <w> <h> [scale] | viewport reset | device <name> | geo <lat> <lng> | offline [on|off] | headers <json>\n  | credentials <user> <pass> | media [dark|light|no-preference] [reduced-motion]\n  | permission geolocation|notifications|camera|microphone|clipboard [more …]\n\nset permission grants those permissions to the tab's site ahead of any prompt (Browser.setPermission: Ardia\n0.2.49 or later; an older one refuses it with policy_refused).\nset viewport sizes the page of a tab this session opened and never showed, for responsive checks. Ardia first takes\nthe tab off-screen (it leaves the sidebar), so an emulated page is never on screen; screenshots, snapshots and\nclicks work there at that size. To see the page, `tab <id> --show` (or `set viewport reset`, in the background):\nthe viewport ends and the page is reloaded in a new tab of its own size. The other settings apply to the session's\ntab where it is. device, geo, offline, headers, credentials and media need Ardia 0.2.49 or later; an older Ardia\nrefuses them (policy_refused), and the error says what to do instead.",
  "state": "ardia browse state save|load <path>\n\nSaves or loads cookies and storage.",
  "dialog": "ardia browse dialog status | accept [text] | dismiss\n\nAnswers the page's open alert, confirm or prompt.",
  "diff": "ardia browse diff snapshot [-b baseline] [-s sel] [-i] [-c] [-d n] | screenshot --baseline <png> | url <u1> <u2>\n\nsnapshot compares with a file, else with this session's last snapshot.",
  "clipboard": "ardia browse clipboard read | write <text> | copy | paste\n\nReads or writes the clipboard through the page, or sends copy or paste to it.",
  "addinitscript": "ardia browse addinitscript <js> [--stdin]\n\nRegisters a script that runs in the page's main world before its own scripts, and prints its id.",
  "text": "ardia browse text [selector]\n\nNot a command of its own: plain `text` runs `read` (the page's text) and `text <selector>` runs `get text <selector>`, each with a one-line note naming that form. Use `read` and `get text` directly.",
  "links": "ardia browse links [pattern] [--limit n]\n\nLists the links on the session's tab whose URL contains the pattern (* or none: every link), in page\norder: read's link listing as one command.",
  "perf": "ardia browse perf trace [--duration 5s] [--reload] [--out <file.json>]\nardia browse perf insight [--reload] [--duration 3s] [--crux]\nardia browse perf insight --trace <file.json>\n\ntrace records a performance trace of the session's tab for --duration (default 5 s; with --reload, from a\nreload until the page loads plus up to 3 s), saves it as JSON (it opens in any trace viewer) and prints its\nanalysis: load metrics, long tasks, main-thread time and render-blocking requests.\ninsight prints load metrics and what to fix: FCP, LCP, CLS, TBT (and TTFB and INP when Ardia analyzes it)\nwith ratings; they need --reload. Ardia records and analyzes it (devtools.perfTrace) when it can, else this\ncommand does. --trace analyzes a saved trace instead (devtools.perfInsight).\n--reload reloads only a tab this session opened (not_own_tab otherwise). --crux adds 28 days of real-user\nfield data for the page's origin from the Chrome UX Report; it is off unless you pass it, needs the API key\nin ARDIA_CRUX_API_KEY, sends only the origin, needs --reload, and is skipped unless Ardia lists the tab (so\nnever for a private window's page, nor over --port or --ws) or when the page changed origin during the trace. Needs Tracing: Ardia 0.2.49 or later.",
  "styles": "ardia browse styles <sel> [--property color,margin-top] [--all]\n\nThe CSS cascade for one element: each property an author rule or the style attribute sets, the value that\nwins, the rule it comes from (selector and stylesheet:line) and the computed value. --all also lists the\ndeclarations it beat (and why, when Ardia answers). --property limits it to those properties, set or not.\nArdia answers it (devtools.styles) when it can, else this command reads the cascade itself. Needs CSS:\nArdia 0.2.49 or later.",
  "issues": "ardia browse issues [--kind cookie,cors,csp,mixed] [--clear]\n\nThe problems DevTools' Issues panel reports for the session's tab, one line each with why and how to fix it:\ncookies the browser dropped or will drop (SameSite, Secure, third-party), requests CORS blocked, Content\nSecurity Policy violations and mixed content. A cookie is named by name and domain, never its value. Ardia\ncollects and explains them (devtools.issues) when it can; else the first call starts collecting (reload the\npage to catch its load-time ones). Needs Audits: Ardia 0.2.49 or later.",
  "logpoint": "ardia browse logpoint add <file:line[:column]> <expression> [--condition <js>] [--column n]\nardia browse logpoint list | rm <id>... | clear | scripts\n\nAdds a logpoint: the page logs the expression's value each time it runs that line, and never pauses. Its\nlines show in `ardia browse console` and start with its label. The file is a script URL (or its end, like\napp.js) or a source file a script's inline (data:) source map names (src/App.tsx): the line is mapped to the\nbundle. --condition logs only when the JavaScript is true; expression and condition run as the page's own\nJavaScript, so keep them to reads. Ardia sets them (devtools.logpoint) when it can, else this command does\nover DevTools. They work only on tabs this session opened, at most 50, and last until rm, clear or the\nsession ends. scripts lists the scripts it can see. Needs Debugger: Ardia 0.2.49 or later.",
  "a11y": "ardia browse a11y [url] [--tags t1,t2] [--selector css]\n\nRuns the bundled accessibility checker on the session's tab (or opens url first) and prints each rule that\nfails, with how many nodes and an example. --tags picks rule sets (wcag2a, wcag2aa, best-practice);\n--selector checks one region.",
  "highlight": "ardia browse highlight <sel>\n\nDraws DevTools' highlight over the element in the session's tab (Overlay.highlightNode). Needs Ardia 0.2.49\nor later; an older one refuses it (policy_refused), so take a `screenshot` there.",
  "pdf": "ardia browse pdf <path> [--landscape]\n\nSaves the session's tab as a PDF (backgrounds printed) to path.",
  "profiler": "ardia browse profiler start [--categories c1,c2] | stop [path]\n\nLike trace, with the profiler's categories: stop saves the events as JSON. Needs Tracing: Ardia 0.2.49 or later.",
  "pushstate": "ardia browse pushstate <url>\n\nChanges the tab's URL with history.pushState, without loading a page (single-page apps route on it).",
  "removeinitscript": "ardia browse removeinitscript <id>\n\nRemoves a script addinitscript registered (the id it printed); pages already loaded keep what it did.",
  "trace": "ardia browse trace start [--categories c1,c2] | stop [path]\n\nRecords a performance trace of the session's tab from start to stop and saves it as JSON. `perf trace`\nrecords one for a set time and summarizes it. Needs Tracing: Ardia 0.2.49 or later.",
  "vitals": "ardia browse vitals [url]\n\nThe page's own timings and web vitals as the page measured them (opens url first when given). For a trace\nwith ratings and what to fix: `perf insight --reload`.",
  "here": "ardia browse here [--read] [--all]\n\nPicks the tab the user is looking at, as `tab active` does, and prints its title, address and what you can\nact on (`snapshot -i`), in one call: the start of any task on the user's own page. --read prints the page's\ntext instead (as `read`); --all lists the whole page when a dialog is open. The session works in that tab\nbut won't navigate it; `open <url>` goes to a background tab of its own.",
  "form": "ardia browse form [<sel>] [-s <sel>] [--all]\n\nLists every visible field on the page (or inside <sel>): text inputs, selects, textareas, checkboxes, radios,\nswitches and editable elements, one line each with its role, label, type, value, checked, required, disabled,\nread-only, the browser's validation message and a ref that fill, select, check and uncheck take:\n  - textbox \"Email\" [type=email, required, invalid: Please fill out this field., ref=e12]: (empty)\n  - radio \"Pro\" [checked=false, group=plan, ref=e15]\nPasswords, card numbers, one-time codes and fields known to be secret show as ••••, and a secret field's\nvalidation message only as `invalid`. --all includes hidden fields. Up to 200 fields.",
  "query": "ardia browse query <sel> [--fields name=<sel>,price=<sel>,url=<sel>@href] [--field k=<sel>] [--limit n] [--all]\n\nReads every visible match of <sel> as one row, so a list of cards or a table is one command:\n  ardia browse query .card --fields name=h2,price=.price,url=a@href\nEach field is a selector inside the match (its text, or a form field's value); `@attr` reads an attribute\n(`@href` and `@src` as full addresses), and `url=@href` reads the match's own. Without --fields, a table\ngives one row per body row keyed by its headers, a row its cells, and anything else its text and first link.\nRows print as JSON lines; --limit sets how many (50 by default, at most 500) and the reading stops there, saying\nwhen more match; a row keeps 30 columns and every value 300 characters; --all includes hidden matches.",
  "read-urls": "ardia browse read-urls <url> [url...] [-s <selector>] [--concurrency n] [--timeout s] [--max-chars n]\n\nFetches up to 50 addresses in parallel from inside this session's tab (the user's active tab when the session\nhas none), with its cookies and logins, without navigating it, and prints each one's text: headings as\nmarkdown, scripts, menus and footers left out. -s / --selector extracts matching elements from each HTML\nresponse before converting to text, and repeated site chrome across 3+ pages is omitted unless --full is set.\nRelative addresses resolve against the tab. Only what the tab's\npage may read works: its own site, and others that allow it; for the rest, the line says to use `read <url>`,\nwhich loads pages in background tabs. Each address gets an equal share of the output cap (--max-chars sets it;\n--full shows all); a failure (HTTP 404, timed out, blocked) is one line and the rest still print.\n--concurrency is 6 by default (at most 10); --timeout is 15 s per address (at most 60). The whole command stops\nwithin 75 s, keeping what finished and marking the rest; bodies stop at 2 MB each and 8 MB in all.",
};
